## Findings


### [E-01] -- Likely Compiler Error: `match` on `usize` with guard arm ordering
Severity: Error (will not compile)
Location: sanity_check(), lines 116-161

    match found_count {
        n if n >= 2 => {}   // guard arm -- FIRST
        1 => { ... }        // specific arm -- SECOND
        _ => { ... }        // catch-all
    }

The problem: found_count is of type usize. In Rust, a guard arm (n if n >= 2) does NOT
contribute to exhaustiveness checking. The compiler cannot prove statically that n >= 2
covers all values >= 2, so it treats the arm as potentially non-exhaustive. Placing the
guarded arm BEFORE the specific literal arm (1 =>) causes the literal arm to be considered
unreachable -- the compiler will likely emit an 'unreachable_patterns' error.

Suggested fix: Replace the match with a plain if/else if/else chain:

    if found_count >= 2 {
        // full confidence, proceed silently
    } else if found_count == 1 {
        // single marker warning + countdown
    } else {
        // no markers -- y/N prompt
    }


### [W-01] -- Warning: ureq HTTP status errors produce raw message strings
Severity: Warning (runtime UX, not a crash)
Location: download_file() lines 366-369; detect_latest_version() lines 171-174

In ureq 2.x, a non-2xx HTTP response (404, 429, etc.) surfaces as
Err(ureq::Error::Status(code, response)). The .with_context() wrapping catches it correctly
and propagates it as an anyhow error -- nothing crashes. However, the failure message the
user sees will be a raw ureq display string such as:
    "HTTP request failed for 'mod.jar': status code: 404"
rather than a cleaner formatted message. This is cosmetic only.

Suggested improvement (post-compilation): Match on ureq::Error::Status(code, _) explicitly
to produce a cleaner message such as: "Server returned HTTP 404 for 'mod.jar'".


### [W-02] -- Warning: splitn(3, '.') silently skips four-part version strings
Severity: Warning (edge case, minor)
Location: parse_csv_version(), lines 200-203

splitn(3, '.') on "3.0.0" correctly yields ["3", "0", "0"].
If a future filename were CreateCore-v3.0.0.1.csv (four-part version), the third split
segment would be "0.1" which fails u32::parse() and returns None -- silently ignoring the
entry. This is acceptable given the vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH naming convention, but should be
documented with an inline comment so future maintainers understand the assumption.

No immediate action required.


### [W-03] -- Warning: Iterating &["mods", "config"] yields &&str items
Severity: Warning (Clippy lint candidate)
Location: offer_backup(), line 275

    for src_name in &["mods", "config"] {

This yields &&str (double reference). It works correctly in practice (Rust auto-derefs
for AsRef<Path> etc.) but may trigger a Clippy lint (needless_borrow or similar).
Cleaner alternatives:
    for src_name in ["mods", "config"] {        // owned array iteration (Rust 2021)
    for src_name in ["mods", "config"].iter() { // explicit iter


### [W-04] -- Warning: read_line() blocks stdin -- acceptable but noted
Severity: Warning (non-interactive usage)
Location: read_line(), lines 453-456

The function blocks waiting for stdin input. In server mode the sanity check and backup
prompt are never reached, so this is a non-issue for the --server path. On the client,
if the binary were ever piped or run non-interactively, it would block indefinitely.
This is standard CLI behaviour and not a bug, but worth noting for future
non-interactive (--yes / --no-backup) flag support.


### [N-01] -- Note: std::process::exit(1) inside a Result-returning function
Severity: Note (design style)
Location: run(), lines 97-99

    if !report.failed.is_empty() {
        std::process::exit(1);
    }
    Ok(()) // <- never reached in the failure case

Functionally correct, but mixing process::exit() inside a Result<()>-returning function
makes line 101 dead code in the failure path. The idiomatic Rust pattern for a CLI tool
is to return ExitCode from main(), or keep all exit() calls only in main(). Style note only.


### [N-02] -- Note: format_unix_utc year loop performance
Severity: Note (no action needed)
Location: format_unix_utc(), lines 461-495

The year-counting loop iterates ~56 times for dates near 2026 (starting from 1970).
Called once per run at program exit. Absolutely negligible. Noted for completeness.


### [N-03] -- Note: LOG_PATH is a relative path -- relies on current_dir() being instance root
Severity: Note (documentation)
Location: constant LOG_PATH line 24; used in append_log() and sanity_check()

The path "logs/createcoreupdater.log" is resolved relative to current_dir() at runtime.
This is correct by the instance-root execution model, but should be explicitly documented
with an inline comment on the constant, as it is load-bearing for both the sanity check
marker detection and the log writer.


